      _push(`<p data-v-f8296f14> Skill-Wanderer began with a charity mindset. Make everything free, give guidance to anyone who asks, and hope goodwill keeps it going. We even built the whole platform on an old laptop and an Orange Pi. </p><p data-v-f8296f14> The intentions were good. But the model depended on things nobody could control: spare time, goodwill, and whether someone would step in to cover the costs. </p></article><article class="chapter" data-v-f8296f14><p class="chapter-label" data-v-f8296f14>Chapter 2</p><h2 data-v-f8296f14><span aria-hidden="true" data-v-f8296f14>⚖️</span> What we learned: good intentions aren&#39;t enough</h2><p data-v-f8296f14> Quality costs something. Human mentors, real projects, servers and tools all take real resources. When help depends on donations, grants or a sponsor&#39;s budget, it can stop at any moment, for reasons that have nothing to do with the people it serves. </p><p data-v-f8296f14> A purely charitable model left us two choices, and neither was good: let the quality slip, or watch the help run out. </p><p class="pull-quote" data-v-f8296f14> If a mission depends on factors beyond its control, the people it serves pay the price when those factors change. </p></article><article class="chapter" data-v-f8296f14><p class="chapter-label" data-v-f8296f14>Chapter 3</p><h2 data-v-f8296f14><span aria-hidden="true" data-v-f8296f14>🌱</span> The shift: entrepreneurship for a social mission</h2>`);
