      _push(`<div class="status-badge" data-v-90f2d486><span data-v-90f2d486>Concept Prototype</span></div><img${ssrRenderAttr("src", _imports_0)} alt="Homepage of the Arlene Rotter concept prototype, with her name, the line I write to ignite, and three of her book covers" class="hero-shot" data-v-90f2d486><h1 data-v-90f2d486>Arlene Rotter</h1><p class="tagline" data-v-90f2d486>Author Website Concept for The Rebel Writer™</p><div class="hero-meta" data-v-90f2d486><span class="meta-tag" data-v-90f2d486>Role: Concept, Design &amp; Development</span><span class="meta-tag" data-v-90f2d486>Author &amp; Novelist</span></div><div class="prototype-banner" data-v-90f2d486><span class="prototype-banner-label" data-v-90f2d486>Concept Prototype</span><p data-v-90f2d486> A free prototype we built for Arlene, shown here with her permission. The books and the writing are hers; the design and the build are ours. It is not her official website and not a client project. </p></div></div></section><section class="detail-section darker" data-v-90f2d486><div class="detail-container" data-v-90f2d486><h2 data-v-90f2d486>Overview</h2><p data-v-90f2d486> Dr. Arlene Rotter spent 41 years in education, earned a Ph.D., founded her own school, and now writes full time as The Rebel Writer™. She has written 25 books and counting, from psychological thrillers and political sci-fi to historical fiction, memoir and Southern humor, and she is seeking literary representation for three finished novels. This prototype shows the website that kind of career needs: a shelf for the published books, a pitch for the new manuscripts, and a way to try her voice before committing to a whole book. </p></div></section><section class="detail-section" data-v-90f2d486><div class="detail-container" data-v-90f2d486><h2 data-v-90f2d486>How It Started</h2><p data-v-90f2d486> I had been reading Arlene&#39;s posts on LinkedIn since the spring of 2026, and as we talked, her goal became clear: to put her books in front of far more readers, with a literary agent behind them. A website could help, but that is easier to show than to explain, so I offered to build a prototype first, with no upfront payment and no obligation to keep it. Two days later it was online, built from her writing, our conversations and a sample chapter she shared with me. </p></div></section><section class="detail-section darker" data-v-90f2d486><div class="detail-container" data-v-90f2d486><h2 data-v-90f2d486>The Challenge</h2><p data-v-90f2d486> An author&#39;s website is read by people who want very different things. A literary agent wants the pitch, the pages and the credentials, fast. A journalist or podcast host wants a bio and an angle. A reader wants to know which book to start with, and whether the voice is for them. Arlene adds a harder problem: she writes across genres on purpose, so a site built around one series or one look would misrepresent her. A standard author template, a grid of covers and a contact page, serves none of these people well. </p></div></section><section class="detail-section" data-v-90f2d486><div class="detail-container" data-v-90f2d486><h2 data-v-90f2d486>Built Around an Agent&#39;s Inbox</h2><div class="highlight-box" data-v-90f2d486><span class="highlight-number" data-v-90f2d486>1 page</span><span class="highlight-label" data-v-90f2d486>with everything a literary agent needs, from logline to press bio</span></div><p data-v-90f2d486> Arlene is seeking representation, so the visitor who matters most is a busy literary agent. Agents get a page of their own: an At a Glance summary written to be read in ninety seconds, the three manuscripts with loglines, synopses and a sample chapter, what she brings to a partnership, her published backlist, and short and full bios to use in pitches. Every manuscript request button opens the contact form with &quot;literary agent&quot; already chosen and the request already written. </p></div></section><section class="detail-section darker" data-v-90f2d486><div class="detail-container" data-v-90f2d486><h2 data-v-90f2d486>What I Built</h2><p class="section-intro" data-v-90f2d486> A 21-page author site where each kind of visitor has a route of its own. </p><div class="features-grid" data-v-90f2d486><div class="feature-card" data-v-90f2d486><div class="feature-icon" data-v-90f2d486>📚</div><h3 data-v-90f2d486>A Shelf by Genre</h3><p data-v-90f2d486> Her books grouped the way readers browse: thrillers and speculative fiction, historical and contemporary fiction, memoir, and education, with the three new manuscripts first. </p></div><div class="feature-card" data-v-90f2d486><div class="feature-icon" data-v-90f2d486>🎨</div><h3 data-v-90f2d486>Covers Set in Type</h3><p data-v-90f2d486> Unpublished manuscripts have no cover art yet, so each one gets a cover set in type, with its own colour and motif, that sits naturally beside the published covers. </p></div><div class="feature-card" data-v-90f2d486><div class="feature-icon" data-v-90f2d486>📄</div><h3 data-v-90f2d486>A Page for Every Book</h3><p data-v-90f2d486> Genre, status, year, format and length, themes, and a content note where one is needed, plus Amazon and Goodreads links for the published titles. </p></div><div class="feature-card" data-v-90f2d486><div class="feature-icon" data-v-90f2d486>📖</div><h3 data-v-90f2d486>A Sample Chapter Reader</h3><p data-v-90f2d486> Chapter One of The Mind Bender Files in one calm column of large serif type, with a reading time and a content note before the first line. </p></div><div class="feature-card" data-v-90f2d486><div class="feature-icon" data-v-90f2d486>🤝</div><h3 data-v-90f2d486>For Agents &amp; Publishers</h3><p data-v-90f2d486> The one-page pitch, with a press kit of short and full bios that agents, editors and event organisers can use as they are. </p></div><div class="feature-card" data-v-90f2d486><div class="feature-icon" data-v-90f2d486>✉️</div><h3 data-v-90f2d486>Contact That Asks Who Is Writing</h3><p data-v-90f2d486> Agents, media and readers each get their own route, so a manuscript request never sits among reader mail. In the prototype the form is a demo and sends nothing. </p></div><div class="feature-card" data-v-90f2d486><div class="feature-icon" data-v-90f2d486>🧭</div><h3 data-v-90f2d486>About: Teacher, Survivor, Writer</h3><p data-v-90f2d486> Her story in three parts: four decades in classrooms, surviving a coma at sixty, and claiming the name The Rebel Writer™. </p></div><div class="feature-card" data-v-90f2d486><div class="feature-icon" data-v-90f2d486>♿</div><h3 data-v-90f2d486>Policies &amp; Accessibility</h3><p data-v-90f2d486> Privacy and cookies, terms, and an accessibility statement aiming at WCAG 2.2 AA, written for her own site and marked as drafts. </p></div></div></div></section><section class="detail-section" data-v-90f2d486><div class="detail-container" data-v-90f2d486><h2 data-v-90f2d486>Inside the Prototype</h2><div class="screens-grid" data-v-90f2d486><figure class="screen screen--wide" data-v-90f2d486><img${ssrRenderAttr("src", _imports_1)} alt="The three manuscripts seeking representation, each with a cover set in type" loading="lazy" data-v-90f2d486><figcaption data-v-90f2d486>The shelf. Three finished novels with no cover art yet, each given a cover set in type.</figcaption></figure><figure class="screen" data-v-90f2d486><img${ssrRenderAttr("src", _imports_2)} alt="The At a glance summary on the page for literary agents" loading="lazy" data-v-90f2d486><figcaption data-v-90f2d486>For agents: the ninety-second version, for a busy inbox.</figcaption></figure><figure class="screen" data-v-90f2d486><img${ssrRenderAttr("src", _imports_3)} alt="Chapter One of The Mind Bender Files in the reader, with a reading time and a content note" loading="lazy" data-v-90f2d486><figcaption data-v-90f2d486>The reader: a reading time and a content note before the first line.</figcaption></figure></div></div></section><section class="detail-section darker" data-v-90f2d486><div class="detail-container" data-v-90f2d486><h2 data-v-90f2d486>Tech Stack &amp; Hosting</h2><div class="tech-grid" data-v-90f2d486><div class="tech-card" data-v-90f2d486><h3 data-v-90f2d486>Astro</h3><span class="tech-label" data-v-90f2d486>Framework</span><p data-v-90f2d486> Static pages and Astro&#39;s image pipeline, which serves covers and photos as responsive WebP in several sizes, so a long shelf of books stays quick on a phone. </p></div><div class="tech-card" data-v-90f2d486><h3 data-v-90f2d486>Tailwind CSS</h3><span class="tech-label" data-v-90f2d486>Design System</span><p data-v-90f2d486> A small palette of paper, ink, ember and brass, defined once as design tokens, so all 21 pages share a look made for this author instead of a stock theme. </p></div><div class="tech-card" data-v-90f2d486><h3 data-v-90f2d486>Self-Hosted Variable Fonts</h3><span class="tech-label" data-v-90f2d486>Typography</span><p data-v-90f2d486> Fraunces for display, Literata for long reading and Inter for the interface, all served from the site itself, with no third-party font requests. </p></div><div class="tech-card" data-v-90f2d486><h3 data-v-90f2d486>Cloudflare</h3><span class="tech-label" data-v-90f2d486>Hosting &amp; Privacy</span><p data-v-90f2d486> Edge hosting on a skill-wanderer.com subdomain, essential cookies only, no analytics, and a noindex tag on every page so the prototype never competes with Arlene&#39;s own presence in search. </p></div></div></div></section><section class="detail-section" data-v-90f2d486><div class="detail-container" data-v-90f2d486><h2 data-v-90f2d486>My Role</h2><p class="section-intro" data-v-90f2d486> Structure first, then the look, then the build: </p><div class="role-list" data-v-90f2d486><div class="role-item" data-v-90f2d486>Shaped the site around its three kinds of visitor: literary agents and publishers, media, and readers.</div><div class="role-item" data-v-90f2d486>Organised her books, credentials and her own words into a shelf, a pitch, and an About page in three parts.</div><div class="role-item" data-v-90f2d486>Designed the visual identity, including covers set in type for the novels that have none yet.</div><div class="role-item" data-v-90f2d486>Built and deployed the 21-page prototype, with responsive images, accessible structure and draft policy pages.</div><div class="role-item" data-v-90f2d486>Marked it as a prototype on every page, with a demo form and noindex tags, so nobody mistakes it for her official site.</div></div></div></section><section class="detail-section darker" data-v-90f2d486><div class="detail-container" data-v-90f2d486><h2 data-v-90f2d486>Why It Is in Our Portfolio</h2><blockquote class="featured-quote" data-v-90f2d486> This is what our free prototype looks like: a working site, made for one person, before any payment or commitment. Compare it with the ones we built for `);
